25.1  How git stores your work

TL;DR
  - A commit is a snapshot of the whole project, plus its author, message and
    parent commit(s). It's named by a hash of all that.
  - A branch is just a name pointing at one commit; committing moves it forward.
    HEAD says which branch you're on.
  - Changes go through three places: your files, the staging area (git add),
    then the repository (git commit).

      git status                    # what changed, what's staged
      git log --oneline --graph --all
      git diff --staged             # what the next commit will contain

Pitfalls
  - "detached HEAD": you checked out a commit, not a branch. New commits there
    belong to no branch: git switch -c new-name keeps them.
  - git add stages a file as it is now; editing it again needs another git add
    before committing.
  - Untracked files aren't protected by git at all: commit or stash before any
    command that cleans the folder.
  - Big binary files bloat every clone forever, even after deletion: keep them
    out, or use Git LFS.

Deep dive

  ▾ What's inside .git?

    An object store: each file version is a blob, each folder a tree listing
    blobs and trees, each commit points to one tree and its parents. All are
    named by the hash of their content, so identical files are stored once and
    any change gives a new name. Branches are small files under .git/refs/heads/
    holding a commit hash; .git/HEAD names the branch.

  ▾ Why are branches cheap?

    Creating one writes a 41-byte file; switching rewrites only the files that
    differ. Nothing is copied. That's why git encourages a branch per feature or
    fix: it costs nothing, and merging is what git is built for.

